{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: during upswings the model reacts too slowly (underestimates the slope). Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 days’ return; amplify it for horizons 7–20 days.",
    "Underpricing of rallies after local lows: when history shows a recent trough followed by stabilization/uptick, the model mean-reverts too hard downward. Reduce mean-reversion strength and bias forecasts upward for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors are modest in the first week but widen materially in weeks 2–4. Use a horizon-scaling correction (e.g., additive premium or multiplicative factor) that ramps up with lead time.",
    "Ceiling effect near higher regimes: when true prices move into the upper 60s, the model tends to cap in mid-60s. If recent prices >~65, shift the whole forecast path upward and allow higher peaks (don’t clip).",
    "Post-spike behavior: after sharp upward moves, the model often predicts partial giveback that doesn’t occur. If last 3–5 days show strong positive returns, dampen any forecasted pullback over the next 5–15 days."
  ]
}
